me><name><surname>Miller</surname><given-names>R.H.</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Density-dependent feedback inhibition of oligodendrocyte precursor expansion</article-title><source>J. Neurosci.</source><volume>16</volume><year>1996</year><fpage>6886</fpage><lpage>6895</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">8824327</pub-id></citation></ref></ref-list><fn-group><fn><p>1.used in this paper: BrdU, bromodeoxyuridine&#x0003b; MBP, myelin basic protein&#x0003b; OPC, oligodendrocyte progenitor cell&#x0003b; PCD, programmed cell death</p></fn></fn-group></back><floats-wrap><fig position="float" id="F1"><label>Figure 1</label><caption><p>NG2 cells colonize the optic nerve in the absence of viable axons. In control nerves, NG2 cells displayed two morphologies: elongated (A) and stellate-shaped (B). Between P0 and P4, NG2 cells increased in a chiasmal-to-retinal gradient. Elongated and stellate cells were quantified per mm<sup>2</sup> of tissue in the chiasmal, mid, and retinal third of longitudinal sections from nerves at P0, P1, P2, P3, and P4 (C). Between P0 and P3, elongated cells (C, black boxes) were enriched at the leading edge of the migrating front, and their processes were always oriented parallel to developing axons (A). The density of NG2 cells increased sixfold between P0 and P4 (C), and stellate-shaped cells comprised 88% of the total NG2 cells at P4. The morphology and density of NG2 cells were determined following optic nerve transection at P0. Both elongated (D) and stellate-shaped (E) NG2 cells were detected in P1-, P2-, P3-, and P4-transected optic nerves, and NG2 cell distribution and density (F) were similar to those detected in P1&#x02013;P4 control nerves. However, the processes extending from these NG2 cells were shorter and irregularly shaped. Bar, 20 &#x003bc;m.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="JCB9904054.f1"/></fig><fig position="float" id="F2"><label>Figure 2</label><caption><p>NG2 cell proliferation indices are similar in control and transected optic nerves. After a 90-min BrdU pulse at P2, the percentages of BrdU and NG2-positive cells were compared in control and P0-transected nerves. Sections were analyzed by confocal microscopy to assure precise identification of BrdU-labeled (A&#x02013;C, red) NG2-positive cells (A&#x02013;C, green). Both elongated and stellate-shaped NG2 cells incorporated BrdU in control (A and B) and transected (C) nerves. Quantification of NG2 and BrdU-positive cells as a percentage of total NG2 cells detected similar (<italic>P</italic> = 0.31) BrdU-labeling indices in control and transected nerves (D). Bar, 20 &#x003bc;m.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="JCB9904054.f2"/></fig><fig position="float" id="F3"><label>Figure 3</label><caption><p>Oligodendrocytes populate the optic nerve in the absence of viable axons. Immunocytochemical detection of MBP-positive oligodendrocytes in sections from control (A, left column) and P4-transected (A, right column) optic nerves at P5, P6, and P7. At the time of transection (P4), oligodendrocytes were restricted to the chiasmal third of the nerve. In P5 control nerves, MBP-positive oligodendrocytes were detected within the chiasmal half of the nerve. P4 transection reduced the number but not the general distribution of MBP-positive cells in P5 nerves. At P6 and P7, the general distribution of MBP-positive oligodendrocytes was similar in control and P4-transected optic nerves. Oligodendrocytes in P6 control nerves (B) and transected nerves (C) occur in clusters of two to three cells. Processes extending from oligodendrocytes appear shorter and less complex in transected nerves at P6. At P7, myelin is abundant in control nerves (D) but not detected in P4-transected (E) nerves. Oligodendrocytes remain in clusters in P7 nerves transected at P4 (E). P4 nerve transection induced a reduction in the density of oligodendrocytes detected at P5, but not at P6 or P7 (F). Bars: 1 mm (A)&#x0003b; 50 &#x003bc;m (B&#x02013;E). Arrows indicate oligodendrocyte cell bodies. Asterisk in F indicates <italic>P</italic> &#x0003c; 0.0001 based on Fisher's test.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="JCB9904054.f3"/></fig><fig position="float" id="F4"><label>Figure 4</label><caption><p>Axonal transection reduces the length and number of premyelinating oligodendrocyte processes. The morphology of premyelinating oligodendrocytes was investigated by confocal microscopy at P6. Premyelinating oligodendrocytes in control sections extended multiple processes that branched extensively (A). Premyelinating oligodendrocytes in transected nerves often occurred in clusters (B) and extend few, short processes. The radial extent of processes was significantly (*<italic>P</italic> &#x0003c; 0.0001) reduced in transected nerves (C). A and B are 1.0-&#x003bc;m-thick optic sections. Asterisks in A and B indicate oligodendrocyte nuclei. Bar, 20 &#x003bc;m.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="JCB9904054.f4"/></fig><fig position="float" id="F5"><label>Figure 5</label><caption><p>Oligodendrocyte PCD is similar in control and transected optic nerves. Sections of control and transected optic nerves were double-labeled with MBP (red) and caspase-3 (green) antibodies and examined by confocal microscopy (A and B). Caspase-3 immunoreactivity was enriched in the cell body of MBP-positive cells. Most (A) but not all (B) MBP caspase-3&#x02013;positive cells had fragmented MBP-stained processes. Compared with control nerves, caspase-3&#x02013;positive cells/mm<sup>2</sup> of tissue (C) were increased in transected nerves at P5 (*<italic>P</italic> &#x0003c; 0.01), but unchanged at P6 and P7 (D). Bars: 10 &#x003bc;m (A and B)&#x0003b; 20 &#x003bc;m (C).</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="JCB9904054.f5"/></fig><fig position="float" id="F6"><label>Figure 6</label><caption><p>OPCs colonize the entire optic nerve and produce oligodendrocytes in the absence of viable axons. Retinas of rats were surgically ablated at P0, and the distribution and density of MBP-positive oligodendrocytes were determined at P7. Compared with normal P7 nerves (A), the loss of viable axons and neuronal electrical activity at P0 had no effect on the distribution of oligodendrocytes in P7 nerves (B). However, the density of oligodendrocytes per mm<sup>2</sup> of tissue (C) was significantly reduced (*<italic>P</italic> &#x0003c; 0.001). Bar, 500 &#x003bc;m.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="JCB9904054.f6"/></fig></floats-wrap></article>